“That was Ron’s interview.”

“Your recorder held one hundred forty-two files before today?”

“Something like that.”

“Something like that gives us no baseline.”

“I clear it all the time.”

“Before or after interviews?”

“Whenever I need space.”

Abby lowered the camera enough to look over it. “Evan.”

He kept his eyes on the display. “What?”

“You said Ron’s interview was the last file.”

“It was.”

“Then one-forty-three would be the next file.”

Diane pressed the menu button with the tip of her pen. The information screen showed the current date and 3:30 p.m. as the file’s creation time. The duration read four seconds.

Evan’s breathing quickened. “The shutdown.”

“The recorder sat here during the shutdown,” Diane said.

“With the screen dark.”

“With its screen dark.”

“You’re doing that on purpose.”

“I’m describing what the camera can support.”

A distant door closed above them. Its impact traveled through the wall, followed by the familiar staff-stair latch. Everyone could identify that source and path. No comparable source or path had yet been identified for the speech-like sound on the recorder.

Diane selected the file.

“State the time,” she said.

Abby checked the camera. “Three thirty-four and fifty-one seconds.”

Diane pressed play.

Hiss filled the small speaker. Under it came a faint impact, then what Diane heard as the first words.

“Come here.”

The voice carried a rough warmth through the electronic thinning. Diane heard Wayne at once: the placement of breath, the upward pull on here, the small pause he used while waiting for attention.

“I have a joke.”

This time, to Diane, the final word emerged.

Evan looked from the recorder to Diane.

She kept her eyes on the display.

The file ended at four seconds. The recorder returned to its menu.

“Again,” Evan said.

Diane played it a second time.

On the second playback, Diane could identify fewer words. The first word could have been a scrape shaped by the hiss. Here might have come from air movement over a microphone. Joke carried only one clear consonant. In four seconds of noise, the mind could complete what it wanted.

To Diane, Wayne’s cadence remained inside it.

Abby whispered, “I hear ‘come here, I have a joke.’”

“You heard Evan say it first,” Diane said.

“I heard ‘come here’ before he said anything.”

“You heard four seconds of noise in a room where we were already discussing Wayne.”

Evan’s attention sharpened. “Those words came from Abby.”

Diane realized the error as soon as the words left her.

Neither spoke for a moment.

Abby kept filming. Her face stayed behind the camera, though one hand tightened around the grip.

Evan rose slowly. “What phrase?”

Diane pressed stop and opened the file information again.

“You just called it a phrase,” he said.

“It is a string of sounds.”

“You said I said it first.”

“You supplied a name. You brought a dead employee into the room. That shapes interpretation.”

“What words do you hear?”

“Nothing reliable.”

Diane answered too quickly and without her usual detail. She could describe every relay, latch, duct, and pressure change in the basement. Faced with four seconds of sound, she offered nothing.

Evan noticed what she had omitted.

“Wayne said that,” he said.

Diane slipped the recorder into the breast pocket of her work shirt.

“It stays with me until administration reviews the footage.”

“That is my property.”

“You brought it into a controlled area and left it unattended during an incident.”

“I want the file copied now.”

“You will receive your equipment when we reach the office.”

Evan stepped between her and the corridor. “You recognized the voice.”

Diane looked past him toward the gray chair.

It stood in the service room beneath the hard ceiling light, FACILITIES tape white around one leg. The seat remained dry. Someone or something had aligned it squarely toward the doorway, though the room revealed no cause. Once Diane noticed that alignment, she could no longer see the chair as incidental.

She unwrapped her keys.

The metal felt warm from her hands.

“Abby,” Diane said, “record the chair before we move it. Full circle. Floor, ceiling, doorway, legs.”

Abby hesitated. “What about the recorder?”

“It is documented.”

Evan remained in Diane’s path. “Answer me.”

“The session is over.”

“You heard Wayne.”

“The session is over.”

Diane’s radio crackled at her shoulder. Ron’s voice arrived through a veil of static.

“Diane, you still below?”

She lifted the radio. “We’re coming up.”

“You need a hand?”

Diane looked at the chair again. One damaged glide. Bare steel. A route with no scratch.

“Yes,” she said. “Meet us at the basement junction.”

Evan turned toward the return grille as Ron’s answer reached them twice, first through Diane’s radio and then through the duct.

“On my way.”

The second version thinned into a distant murmur.

Evan pointed at the grille. “That voice carried from upstairs. The recorder voice came from the device.”

“Correct.”

“So stop pretending everything is a vent.”

“I gave you an example of known transmission. I made no claim about the file.”

“You’re hiding the claim.”

“I’m preserving the evidence from your claim.”

Diane entered the service room.

The chair occupied the exact center between the rolling whiteboards and the stacked bins. She approached from behind, keeping its seat visible to Abby’s camera. No moisture marked the plastic. The tape around the leg remained tight. She tilted the chair and showed the underside.

A dark bead clung to the rear crossbar.

Diane touched it with the tip of her pen.

Water gathered around the plastic cap and slipped onto the floor.

Abby moved closer.

“Stay at the doorway,” Diane said.

The bead struck the wax and formed a round mark no wider than a dime.

Diane examined the crossbar. The hollow metal tube had a small vent hole near the weld. She tilted the chair farther. Another drop appeared.

“Water inside the frame,” she said.

“From where?” Abby asked.

“The chair was wet yesterday. Water could have entered through the vent hole.”

“Then it was wet in the lobby,” Evan said.

“The seat was dry. The frame remained unchecked.”

The explanation accounted for part of the object’s condition without resolving Diane’s unease. Water could remain trapped in tubular steel and escape hours after a chair left a damp location. The first moisture still lacked a source. The route from lobby to basement remained equally open.

Diane lifted the chair by its back and front edge.

“Keep filming.”

She carried it into the corridor. The exposed glide stayed above the floor. Evan followed near her shoulder, still arguing about the recorder.

At bay C, Diane placed the chair beside the five tagged repairs.

Six again.

She removed the FACILITIES tape and restored the orange repair tag from a roll inside the cage. The ordinary act felt ceremonial under Abby’s camera.

“Why bring it back here?” Evan asked.

“It belongs here.”

“What if it moves again?”

“Then its location changes again.”

“What if the recorder plays again?”

“Then the device produces another file.”

“What would convince you?”

Diane closed the cage door.

“Reliable observation. Known controls. Repeatable conditions. Complete access records. Original files. Independent review.”

“We have most of that.”

“We have one camera angle, incomplete access coverage, an unattended recorder, and three people carrying prior information.”

“We have Wayne’s voice.”

Diane threaded the padlock through the hasp.

“You have a four-second recording.”

“That you recognized.”

The lock closed under her thumb.

A soft click came from her shirt pocket.

All three heard it.

Diane froze.

The recorder’s display glowed through the fabric, a dim blue rectangle over her heart.

Evan’s expression changed from accusation to wonder.

“Take it out,” Abby said.

Diane removed the device and held it where the camera could see.

A new file counter ran across the screen.

00:01.

00:02.

00:03.

The speaker remained silent. The recorder was capturing.

Diane pressed stop.

The file saved as one hundred forty-four.

Evan reached toward it, then stopped himself. “Play it.”

“Upstairs.”

“Now.”

“We move to the office, copy both files, record device settings, and contact the assistant principal.”

“You think an office makes it safer?”

“I think an office adds witnesses.”

Footsteps approached from the west stair. Ron’s keys accompanied them, a loose practical jingle with one heavy brass key arriving after the others.

Diane faced the sound.

Ron entered the corridor and slowed when he saw the camera, the locked cage, and the recorder in Diane’s hand.

“What happened?”

Evan answered first. “We recorded Wayne.”

Diane looked at him.

He had stated a possibility as fact.

Ron’s face lost color. His gaze went to Diane rather than the device.

She gave the smallest shake of her head.

Evan caught it.

The motion lasted less than a second.

Evan lowered his voice. “You know what he sounded like.”

Ron looked between them. “Pack up.”

“We need to hear the new file,” Evan said.

Ron stepped into the corridor until his body filled the route to the boiler room. “Pack up.”

Abby lowered the camera a few inches. Its red light remained on.

Diane placed the recorder in her pocket and handed Ron the chair-cage key.

“Lock stays closed until morning. Record the time in the shift log.”

Ron took the key. “Three thirty-eight.”

“Include the students’ names, my name, the camera, and the recorder.”

Evan kept his eyes on Diane. Fear had receded from his face. He no longer asked what the recorder contained. He watched her for confirmation.

Diane said nothing.

He had already called the recording Wayne.

# **Scene 5 — The Markers**

Ron held out his hand for the recorder.

Diane kept it in her pocket.

“We document it upstairs,” she said.

Evan moved between her and the staff stair. “You take it upstairs, the file disappears into an administrator’s inbox.”

“The file stays on your device.”

“The device stays with you.”

“Until we have witnesses and a copy.”

Abby lowered the camera enough to expose her face. “A copy here would protect everybody.”

Diane looked at her. Abby’s reasoning held more weight than Evan’s urgency. One file on one student recorder established no chain of custody. The group could only dispute who had touched it. A copy made under continuous camera would preserve the device state and remove Evan’s strongest argument for remaining below.

“One copy,” Diane said. “No edits. Camera stays continuous. Ron stands beside me.”

Evan unzipped the camera bag before she finished.

He brought out a thin laptop, a card reader, two short cables, and a pair of headphones. With the equipment arranged, the junction became a temporary media station. Abby placed the camera on its tripod with the shelf, electrical clock, laptop, and all four people inside the frame. Ron remained near the boiler-room fire door. His keys hung motionless against his jacket.

Diane checked the clock.

3:40.

“State every connection,” she said.

Evan plugged the recorder into the laptop. “USB data cable from recorder to computer. Recorder storage mounted as external drive.”

A file window opened. Rows of numbered audio clips filled the screen.

“Show the camera the directory before moving anything.”

He turned the laptop toward Abby. The newest entries read 00143 and 00144\. File 143 lasted four seconds and had a creation time of 3:30 p.m. File 144 lasted three seconds and had a creation time of 3:37 p.m.

Diane pointed with her pen. “Copy both into a new folder. Preserve the original names.”

Evan created a folder on the desktop.

He typed WAYNE BASEMENT.

“Change it,” Diane said.

“That identifies the incident.”

“It assigns an identity before review.”

He looked at her over the screen. “You assigned it when you recognized him.”

“Change it.”

Abby said, “Use date and location.”

Evan erased the name and typed BELLWEATHER\_BASEMENT\_01.

The cursor blinked after the final digit.

Diane watched Evan finish the label. Even a neutral folder joined the files and influenced how later reviewers would encounter them.

